## 8. Final Expected Outcome

I want MESH OFFGRID and XFER to behave like reliable, production-grade device-to-device communication systems.

**MESH OFFGRID:** Once two Android devices establish a Bluetooth connection, they should be able to continuously exchange messages in both directions, see replies immediately, and maintain a stable communication session.

**XFER:** Once two devices establish a Wi-Fi-based connection, they should remain connected, exchange transfer requests and notifications immediately, and display synchronized file transfer progress and results on both devices.

**Most importantly, both devices must always agree on the actual connection, session, messaging, and transfer status.**

Please perform a complete audit, identify the root causes, implement the necessary backend, transport-layer, Rust, Android, and frontend fixes, and validate the results through actual physical-device testing.

Do not mark this task as completed merely because the code compiles or the application builds successfully. Completion requires verified end-to-end functionality on both physical Android devices.


My additional technical recommendation: Pay special attention to the distinction between device discovery, transport connection, and application session. These are three separate lifecycle stages. Your symptoms suggest that the application may be treating discovery and connection as the same state, or recreating transport sessions when new messages or transfer events arrive.

For QGUARD HELIX, the ideal architecture should maintain a persistent session manager for each connected peer, with independent messaging and file-transfer event handling. This will help prevent one operation from accidentally terminating another.